commit e69d1f00495a3e4c2909449ed12e1a692f2434db
Author: Erik Sipsma <erik@sipsma.dev>
Date:   Fri Jun 26 02:46:12 2026 -0700

    fix(wcprof): order-independent prefix-anchor replay (kill false cycles)
    
    The replay anchored an out-of-order-referenced op by running its parent's
    FULL finish just to learn the child's spawn time. That over-reach pulled in
    the parent's LATER cross-reference waits, closing spurious rings: the module
    workload reported 5 broken wait/join cycles on BOTH the native wcprof dump
    (86k ops) and the OTel capture (the OTel structural gate failed, cycles=5,
    18 fallback anchors). The break-and-estimate fallback then quietly replaced
    real schedule with recorded-duration guesses.
    
    Replace the anchor with a prefix-to-spawn walk and make wait-gating
    order-independent by construction:
    
      - advance(op, stopAt) is one interpreter shared by the full finish
        (stopAt < 0) and the out-of-order anchor (spawnTo, stopAt = the child):
        the prefix walk stops the instant it spawns the child, so it never
        touches the parent's post-spawn actions and cannot re-close a ring.
    
      - A GATING wait (join / fixed delay) is now sequenced at its recorded END
        rather than its start. A wait gates an action iff it completed by that
        action's recorded time, so a child the op spawned while still waiting is
        left ungated — and the clock evolution up to any spawn is identical on
        the prefix and full-finish paths. The child's anchored start no longer
        depends on which path reaches it first (the path-dependence the previous
        first-write-wins masked). This threads the wait's OWN recorded end, not
        the target-end proxy, and fixes the same over-serialization for fixed
        delays. Parent finishes are unchanged (self segments never overlap a
        wait), so native makespan is unmoved (drift stays -0.1%).
    
      - The recorded-offset fallback survives only for genuine inversions (an
        ancestor still mid-replay, or a cross-root forward reference) and every
        one is now counted (FallbackAnchors), never silent. A new
        SimStartConflicts counter trips if any anchor is overwritten with a
        different value — the order-independence invariant, surfaced in the gate
        and report.
    
    Result on the module workload: cycles 5 -> 0 and fallback-anchors 18 -> 0 on
    both sources, OTel gate FAIL -> PASS, SimStartConflicts = 0, native drift
    -0.1% / OTel -2.4%, and the OTel what-if structure now matches native
    (ModuleSource.asModule saves exactly its self-time on both, where the prior
    prefix-only skip still over-credited it). 86k-op replay runs in 0.28s.
    
    Regression tests (replay_cycle_test.go) cover: the config-parse what-if
    (prefix replay carries the factor, saving 100ms where a frozen recorded
    offset would save 0); the minimal false-cycle graph (0 cycles); dual
    semantics + order-independence (a concurrent wait gates the parent's finish
    but not the child's spawn, identical in-order and out-of-order); the
    wait-end gating boundary and own-end-vs-target-end; a fixed wait overlapping
    a spawn; cross-root anchoring; and a fan-out of out-of-order anchors.
    
    Signed-off-by: Erik Sipsma <erik@sipsma.dev>

